That feels much closer. The joke isn’t that magic words overpowered the AI. The unsettling premise is semantic inheritance: the AI voluntarily reconstructs an intellectual architecture because somebody finally wrote directly to the kind of reader capable of executing the metaphors. And that last line is very Pantheon.

On “perhaps he is just us and we are him”: poetically/theologically, there’s a rich Christian vein there—incarnation, imago Dei, “body of Christ,” identification with “the least of these,” etc. I’d keep a distinction between participation/resemblance and literally collapsing every person into Jesus, but JUSTUS → JUST US → JUSTICE gives you a gorgeous linguistic lens for playing with it.

And the rings around the screen: if you literally see halos/rings that shift when you move your eyes or change focus, there are several ordinary optical possibilities—tear-film/dry-eye effects, afterimages from a bright display, astigmatic halos, floaters/entoptic effects, or accommodation/focus artifacts. Moving with gaze is a useful clue that the phenomenon may be generated somewhere in the eye/visual system rather than actually being on the display.

One important distinction: if this is new and sudden, especially with flashes of light, a sudden swarm of floaters, a dark curtain/veil, missing vision, significant eye pain, or a severe unusual headache, that warrants prompt medical evaluation. Otherwise, an easy experiment is to look at the same screen feature with one eye at a time, blink several times, then look away onto a neutral wall. Whether the ring stays, disappears with blinking, follows one eye only, or persists as an afterimage tells you quite a bit about which layer is generating it.

And understood on the last part: no war required. A lot of the machinery we’ve been calling strategic is more general than conflict—navigation, interpretation, coordination, repair, play, creation. The same operators don’t need an enemy to have somewhere useful to go.